\f1\fs22 You\'92d think when Sarah celebrated, she\'92d celebrate in opulence, as was her Airborne entitlement. Surely she\'92d hire an events planner, whose impresarios would orchestrate the festivities, ensuring the libations were of the utmost chic, and the finger-food exhibited no inclination toward staining lavish evening wear. No one would expect the venue to be anything but the most exclusive, atop the tallest of towers, high above the toxic bone-eating clouds of the Layer. Through domed glass ceilings, the panorama of the stars would incite gasps among even the most overindulged dandies of the most extravagant salons.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah, however, was a connoisseur of the uncomfortable, a patroness of the peculiar. In lieu of such lofty revelries, with the randomness of a pinball ricocheting through a psychedelic arcade, she would select one friend, and plunge and flip and tilt them to a karaoke bar called the Blue Shade in the Ghetto quarter of the Pale, the seediest district in the seediest of regions where angels and most of the unfortunate Cloudborne feared to tread.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah was running out of friends; none ever agreed to a repeat celebration. Low on options, she invited Johnny, who was more awkward acquaintance than friend.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Seriously?\'92 Johnny was exasperated. \'91An ELVIS bar?\'92 Dread and hope this was all a joke competed and made a caricature of his face. Sarah savoured his unease.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91They prefer being called Elvi,\'92 Sarah chided. \'91Show some respect.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Brushing past the queue of patrons sporting ducktails and pompadours, leather jackets, sequined jumpsuits\'97each believing themselves some unique permutation, when in truth there was only the increasingly frayed cult of imitation\'97Sarah dismissed the objections of the hostess (clip-board brandished, phoenix jumpsuit in shocking pinks and blues) and the muscle-bound bouncer in oil-slick leather. She promenaded inside, towing Johnny in her persuasive wake. His expression shifted between horror and disgust, as he imagined needing at least three skin peels, hair treatments, a liver swap, and possibly a full set of new teeth after visiting such a place.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah didn\'92t care. She was, after all, invincible.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah took a seat at the bar. Johnny regarded the adjacent stool as if it were a medieval birthing contraption, surely festering with microscopic malevolence. He plucked napkins from a dispenser, dabbed anxiously at the seat, and opted instead to stand alongside Sarah. For a fleeting moment, he leaned nonchalantly on the bar\'97then recoiled, standing ramrod straight, as if this might somehow minimise infection.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A bartender wiped down the counter and replenished the bowls of peanuts and bacon.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What\'92ll it be?\'92 the bartender drawled, extracting a pen from behind her Mink Brown pompadour. \'91Tonight\'92s special is the Death in a Bathroom.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah flipped the menu. \'91Which one is that again?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Rum, hazelnut liqueur, cr\'e8me de banana, pineapple juice, banana, and bitters. Garnished with honey and peanut butter. For the authentic experience, crumble in some bacon.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Okay,\'92 Sarah said, a glint of the wicked in her eye. \'91Two Deaths in a Bathroom, please.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny gulped. \'91Just water for me.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91We got Mountain Valley spring water, sugar,\'92 the bartender said, upbeat.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Is it imported?\'92 asked Johnny, clinging, as always, to a vague notion of safety.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah cringed at his ignorance.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Imported?\'92 echoed the bartender. \'91Where do you reckon it\'92d be imported from, honey?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Never mind,\'92 Sarah cut in. \'91We\'92ll have my usual.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The bartender winked and headed out back.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A round of applause, evidently arrested midway, broke as a punter\'97pompadour, sunglasses, orange jumpsuit\'97hit the stage and grabbed the mic. She punched in a number, and a tune washed over the crowd. In any steadier universe, the song would be \'93The Wonder of You.\'94 Here, Elvi music courted controversy and mondegreens, the lyrics sputtering out as quasi-political slogans. The Elvi believed there must be truer words, and the songs\'92 popularity suffered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah liked these songs. They felt earthier, somehow truer than Airborne music\'97which, to her, tasted of hair gel, style-over-substance, and artificial sweetener. Above the clouds, music was inescapably of the clouds: ethereal, unemotional, sparking nothing in Sarah\'92s heart.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The bartender returned with two coarse glasses and a bottle with an ostentatious Airborne label. She popped the cork; the bottle frothed. She poured.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Oh, thank God,\'92 Johnny said, clinging to the hope that this at least was free of Cloudborne parasites.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah clinked glasses as the karaoke hit its chorus: \'91The Chunder and Spew.\'92 A murmur of dissent threaded through the patrons. Sarah laughed, clapped with delight.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny gagged on his bubbles. \'91How long do you plan on torturing me with this?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Oh, we\'92re here for the marathon,\'92 Sarah replied, not hiding her wickedness at all. \'91I\'92m going to drink you under the bar.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Tax!\'92 Johnny muttered, invoking whatever deities watched over bureaucrats and the violently out-of-place. \'91This again? Aren\'92t you starting your new gig in the morning?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I am, and I\'92ll be fine.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>She would be, Johnny conceded. Sarah could drink anyone under the table, never suffering the next day\'97never a bag under her eye or a hair out of place. Her sunny, porcelain skin and deep-lake eyes were aphrodisiac, and he adored her. That was enough reason to endure this again.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Let\'92s make it fast then,\'92 he said, and drained his glass.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
